1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,133
Meet Breach Responder: an AI Skill for UGREEN AI NAS.

2
00:00:05,733 --> 00:00:14,133
So, you get an email: Acme Insurance has had a data breach. And your first thought is… wait, what did I actually give them?

3
00:00:14,733 --> 00:00:22,800
Years of forms, invoices and screenshots are sitting on your NAS. Digging through all of that by hand? That takes hours.

4
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:36,667
Breach Responder answers using only the files on your NAS. Nothing leaves the device — you’d never upload your passport scans to a cloud AI just to check.

5
00:00:37,267 --> 00:00:44,567
You just type the company name, any other names you know it by, and the folders it’s allowed to search. That’s it.

6
00:00:45,167 --> 00:00:54,633
First, it searches three ways at once: by file name, by what’s written inside your documents, and by the text in your photos and screenshots.

7
00:00:55,233 --> 00:01:05,867
So it catches files like INV-554.pdf and scan_004.jpg — not just the ones with “Acme” in the name.

8
00:01:06,467 --> 00:01:14,367
Next, it reads each file and lists which types of personal data show up, each with its source file and a confidence level.

9
00:01:14,967 --> 00:01:23,433
And it never shows the actual card or passport number. Not even the last four digits. Just the type of data, and where it lives.

10
00:01:24,033 --> 00:01:34,767
Anything unclear or unreadable goes into a “needs a human look” list instead of being guessed. If nothing matches, it simply says so.

11
00:01:35,367 --> 00:01:41,600
Then you get a checklist, highest risk first, with every item tied to the file behind it.

12
00:01:42,200 --> 00:01:49,733
The report is only saved to your NAS after you approve it. Nothing gets moved, and nothing gets deleted.

13
00:01:50,333 --> 00:01:55,300
Breach Responder. Your data, your answer, on your NAS.
